Item 01 is done and the gate passes. Over everything I could read, I found no earlier report that Chu & Stuckey's definite move or better move is wrong, and nobody has published a correction.

**What was swept.** OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar and OpenCitations list 144 distinct works that cite either the CP 2009 paper, Chu's 2011 thesis, or the thesis's IJCAI 2013 abstract.
- **CP paper:** 38 real citers. 24 of them (63%) were read in full; for the rest no full text could be found, open or behind a paywall.
- **Thesis or abstract only:** 103 citers, 32 of them (31%) read in full. Almost all cite it for the Chuffed solver or lazy clause generation, not for the open-stacks rules.

Each full text was searched for the rules and the theorem numbers. Every passage that mentions open stacks or cites the paper was then read in context.

**What turned up:**
- **One more restatement.** Fink (2012), a PhD thesis at ICMC-USP, restates Theorem 1 (the definite move) on pp. 27–28, with the published premise, as true. Our counterexample graph refutes it too. I saved it as `literature/fink_2012_phd_thesis_mosp_novas_contribuicoes.pdf`. Nobody restates the better move.
- **Three works use an implementation of the rules, and none reports a failure.**
  - Chu, Garcia de la Banda & Stuckey (2010, 2012) build them into a constraint-programming model as dominance-breaking constraints.
  - Frinhani et al. (2018) ran the original C code to get their published optima, and Martin, Yanasse & Pinto (2022) reuse those values.
- **A related observation, not about the premise.** Beck, Kuroiwa, Lee, Stuckey & Zhong (CP 2025, Prop. 3 and Example 6) warn that two moves which dominate each other can make a satisfiable instance look unsatisfiable. That is the general form of our earlier `better_move` cycle bug.

**Unread.** 14 citers of the CP paper had no copy I could get, plus Gange, Chu & Stuckey (2019), whose author page blocked the fetch. They are listed in `literature/MISSING.md`. The one to fetch first is De Giovanni et al. (2013, *ITOR*), because it is an exact method for gate matrix connection cost and the most likely to reuse the rules. Two other unread entries look like false index links: both are *ITOR* papers on efficiency analysis.

**Where it is:**
- The table, one row per citer, and the closing verdict are in `paper2/prior_art_counterexample.md`, under "Every citer, swept". I also added a one-line pointer at the top of that file.
- `python3 -m paper2.prior_art_sweep` regenerates `paper2/data/prior_art/citers.csv` and `hits.txt`.
- Downloaded PDFs are in `paper2/data/prior_art/fulltext/`, which is git-ignored (about 55 MB). Every URL fetched by hand is recorded in the script.

**Checks.** The gate passed: Lean builds with its one known `sorry`, 1,361 MOSP tests and 114 pathwidth_solver tests pass. The split run's log has no `!!!` line. No published claim of ours needed fixing. PROGRESS.md is updated to 1/9 and the item is marked `[x]`.

